1. Lean Waste and Remedies

i) Waiting & Handoffs
The Process View and Development View depend on the Logical View, which is represented by the Class Diagram. While the diagram was still in progress, they were unable to proceed with their tasks because they were waiting for the results.
As a result, only the Logical View task was progressing during that period, while the members working on the dependent views were idle. This meant that the tasks could not be done at the same time, which reduced the time left to complete the remaining views and review and refine the diagrams.
To reduce this waste, the member responsible for the Class Diagram can share partial drafts periodically via WhatsApp chat group or GitHub. The other members can then begin their work from the draft and refine their views as the diagram is updated without waiting for the final version. When the diagram changes, the member should announce changes in the group chat. This allows the group to reduce external handoff waiting.

ii) Defects and Rework
During the review, the Sequence Diagram and the Use Case Diagram did not match the Class Diagram. For example, the method viewReward() appeared in the Use Case Diagram and the Sequence Diagram but was missing from the Class Diagram. This happened because changes made to one diagram were overlooked by the other members, so the related diagrams were not updated.
As a result, the diagrams were inconsistent with one another. This meant that members had to correct the affected diagrams, which added extra effort and reduced the time available for other tasks.
To reduce this waste, the group can treat the Class Diagram as the main reference for all other diagrams. Before finalising, a member should check every method and actor in the other diagrams against it. Any change should be announced in the group chat and committed in the shared repository. This allows the group to detect inconsistencies early and reduce rework.

iii) Defects and Rework
Case-specific example:
The rewards requirement in the assignment instructions was initially overlooked, as it was assumed to be unnecessary. The reward feature had even been removed from the Sequence Diagram and Use Case Diagram because it was missing from the Class Diagram. When the requirement was noticed, the group had to add viewReward() back and adjust the related diagrams.
Impact:
Diagrams that had already been completed had to be reopened, which increased the amount of rework.
Remedy:
Read the full instructions together at the start and list every required feature in a checklist, then tick each one off against the diagrams before submission.

iv) Overprocessing
Case-specific example:
The instructions asked for one diagram per view, yet the group discussed whether to add a Data Flow Diagram, noting that other groups had drawn more than one. The Process View also ended up with two diagrams, which increased the page count and required adjustments to image sizes and layout.
Impact:
The extra diagram and the discussion took attention away from checking the required diagrams. The additional length also required formatting changes to keep the report within the page limit.
Remedy:
Decide the number of diagrams per view from the instructions before drawing anything, and add an extra diagram only if it covers something the required one cannot.

